// kama call sites are LABEL-based, so a parameter's label is part of the call surface a conformance
// promises — not decoration. Let it diverge and one operation gets two spellings depending on how the
// receiver is held (`w.put(bytes:)` through the contract, `w.put(data:)` on the type), and any generic
// body written against the contract fails to compile for THIS implementer, reported in the generic's
// own file, naming a label its author wrote correctly.
//
// Measured at zero divergences across lib/prelude/tests/examples/bench before the rule was turned on:
// the convention was already universal, so the rule costs nothing and only stops it from rotting.
type contract Writer2 for value { fn int32 put(int32 bytes); }
type value W implements Writer2 {
    public ctor make() { }
    public fn int32 put(int32 data) { return data; }     // the contract calls it `bytes` -> REJECT
}
fn int32 main() { W w = W.make(); return w.put(data: 0); }
